Almost. Since `location.pathname` is already normalized, use a regex literal and anchor it:

```js
(() => {
  const isContentMigrations =
    /^\/courses\/\d+\/content_migrations\/?$/.test(location.pathname);

  if (isContentMigrations) {
    console.log("you are here.");
  }
})();
```

Your current regex requires a trailing `/`, so it won’t match `/courses/163/content_migrations` exactly. Use this variant if nested paths should also match:

```js
/^\/courses\/\d+\/content_migrations(?:\/|$)/
```

`URLPattern` is a modern alternative, but this simple anchored regex is clearer and more broadly compatible.

Only if you need the course ID later.

```js
const match = /^\/courses\/(?<courseId>\d+)\/content_migrations\/?$/.exec(location.pathname);

match && console.log(`Course: ${match.groups.courseId}`);
```

If you only need a true/false route check, a named capture group adds unnecessary complexity:

```js
/^\/courses\/\d+\/content_migrations\/?$/.test(location.pathname) && console.log("you are here.");
```

Use an IIFE for the chained callback:

```js
/^\/courses\/\d+\/content_migrations\/?$/.test(location.pathname) &&
  (() => {
    const inputs = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
    console.log(inputs);
  })();
```

The `{ ... }` after `&&` is parsed as an object expression, so it cannot contain `const`. For anything beyond a tiny action, `if` is clearer:

```js
if (/^\/courses\/\d+\/content_migrations\/?$/.test(location.pathname)) {
  const inputs = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
  console.log(inputs);
}
```

A `MutationObserver` is the right choice because the options are inserted asynchronously after the click. Observe the page before the user interacts:

```js
(() => {
  if (!/^\/courses\/\d+\/content_migrations\/?$/.test(location.pathname)) return;

  const allowed = new Set([
    "empty",
    "canvas_cartridge_importer",
    "course_copy_importer",
  ]);

  const removeUnwantedOptions = () => {
    document
      .querySelectorAll('ul[role="listbox"] li[role="none"] > span[role="option"][value]')
      .forEach((option) => {
        if (!allowed.has(option.getAttribute("value"))) {
          option.closest("li")?.remove();
        }
      });
  };

  const observe = () => {
    removeUnwantedOptions();

    new MutationObserver(removeUnwantedOptions).observe(document.body, {
      childList: true,
      subtree: true,
    });
  };

  if (document.body) {
    observe();
  } else {
    document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", observe, { once: true });
  }
})();
```

This removes the entire `<li>` containing each disallowed option and preserves the three allowed values. It also handles the list being recreated later.

Yes. `:has()` can handle this cleanly, and CSS automatically applies to options inserted later:

```js
(() => {
  if (!/^\/courses\/\d+\/content_migrations\/?$/.test(location.pathname)) return;

  const style = document.createElement("style");

  style.textContent = `
    ul[role="listbox"] > li[role="none"]:has(
      > span[role="option"][value]
        :not([value="empty"])
        :not([value="canvas_cartridge_importer"])
        :not([value="course_copy_importer"])
    ) {
      display: none !important;
    }
  `;

  document.head.append(style);
})();
```

Your proposed `:not([value="none"])` would exclude only an option whose value is literally `"none"`. Since you need to preserve three specific values, chain `:not()` for those three allowed values.

This hides the unwanted items rather than removing them from the DOM. For visual filtering, that’s simpler and handles dynamically inserted options without a `MutationObserver`.

Then replace only the final line:

```js
o();
```

Your existing structure is fine. The full ending becomes:

```js
const o = () => {
  ro();
  new MutationObserver(ro).observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });
};

o();
```

Since the IIFE runs after `document.body` exists, the `document.body ? ... : ...` conditional and `DOMContentLoaded` listener are unnecessary.

Because `ro()` performs the immediate cleanup, while the `MutationObserver` handles future changes:

```js
const o = () => {
  ro(); // Remove options already present in the DOM.

  new MutationObserver(ro).observe(document.body, {
    childList: true,
    subtree: true,
  }); // Remove options inserted later.
};
```

Without the first `ro()`, any options already rendered before your script runs would remain until another mutation occurs. Since the observer only reacts to mutations after registration, the initial cleanup ensures the current DOM is filtered immediately.
